<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pandulf IV of Capua</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1050</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Wolf of the Abruzzi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pandulf IV was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> prince of Capua who ruled at intervals between 1016 and 1050. Called the Wolf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzi</a>, he plundered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> and fought the princes of Salerno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, hiring Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> who later carved out lordships of their own. The emperor Conrad II deposed him in 1038, but he regained Capua in 1047.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pandulf-iv-of-capua.jpg" alt="Pandulf IV of Capua" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiser_Heinrich_II._l%C3%A4%C3%9Ft_F%C3%BCrst_Pandulf_IV._von_Capua_gefangensetzen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Pandulf IV ruled first from February 1016 jointly with his cousin Pandulf II. After the Byzantine catapan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-boioannes">Basil Boioannes</a> destroyed the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melus-of-bari">Melus of Bari</a> and his Norman allies at Cannae in 1018, Pandulf became the most ardent of the southern princes in support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, and in 1020 he helped Boioannes take the tower of Melus' brother-in-law Dattus on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garigliano">Garigliano</a>. This brought an imperial army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Pilgrim, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, besieged Capua and took the prince in 1022; Emperor Henry II almost executed him, and he was imprisoned in Germany for two years.</p><p>Emperor Conrad II released him in 1024 at the request of Prince Guaimar III of Salerno. With Guaimar, the Norman adventurer Rainulf Drengott and later Boioannes, Pandulf besieged Capua, which fell in 1026 after a siege of 18 months. In 1027 he deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergius-iv-of-naples">Sergius IV of Naples</a>, but Rainulf restored Sergius in 1029 and received the county of Aversa, the first Norman toehold in the Mezzogiorno. Pandulf imprisoned Theobald, the abbot of Montecassino, and in 1032 conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a> from John V and took its ruler's titles. The chronicler Aimé of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> called him a man of &quot;wily and wicked deeds&quot;.</p><p>At the request of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaimar-iv-of-salerno">Guaimar IV of Salerno</a>, Conrad came south and at Troia in 1038 ordered Pandulf to restore stolen property to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>. Pandulf offered 300 lbs of gold and a son and daughter as hostages, but when the son escaped he took refuge in his castle of Sant'Agata de' Goti, and Conrad gave Capua to Guaimar. Pandulf fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, seeking the protection of his old Byzantine allies, but was imprisoned there, and he was released only after Guaimar became an enemy of Emperor Michael IV.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Pandulf returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1042, and for five years he and his few followers threatened Guaimar. In 1047, a watershed year in the history of the Mezzogiorno and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, Emperor Henry III, Conrad's son, came south, made the Drengot and Hauteville possessions his direct <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, and restored Pandulf at Capua for the last time. Pandulf died in his own princedom, after a third reign.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pandulf IV of Capua” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Pandulf IV</b>, called the Wolf of the Abruzzi, despoiled Monte Cassino and, like his rivals, hired Norman mercenaries, helping the Normans gain a foothold in Campania.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-capua">Principality of Capua</a></li></ul></section>
